 5. 🫏 Dems reroute road to nomination 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 DNC member Donna Brazile attends a Black Caucus meeting Thursday in Austin. Photo: Joel Angel Juarez/AP For 52 years — 14 presidential elections — Iowa and New Hampshire hosted the nation's first nominating contests. No longer. The Democratic National Committee's summer meeting in Austin yesterday ratified a wildly different calendar. Sorry, Iowa! Here are Dems' six contests before Super Tuesday: 

 South Carolina: Jan. 22, 2028. Nevada: Feb. 1, 2028. New Hampshire: Feb. 8, 2028. New Mexico: Feb. 15, 2028. Michigan: Feb. 22, 2028. Virginia: Feb. 29, 2028. Between the lines: Iowa and New Hampshire, the launch states starting in 1972 , are around 90% white . The new schedule gives more voice to Black and Latino voters, as well as union members.

 🥊 Reality check: It's not over. Iowa Democrats have indicated they could go rogue and hold a caucus when Republicans do, regardless of DNC penalties, Axios' Alex Thompson notes. 

 The New Hampshire Democratic Party has argued it can't change the state law mandating the Granite State hold the first primary contest. Both a Republican governor and state Democrats look unwilling to bend. 🔮 What's next: New Hampshire has until mid-September to submit a plan to comply with the DNC's calendar. If neither Iowa nor New Hampshire complies, we'll see what penalties the DNC considers for states and potential candidates.

 Go deeper . President Trump dances Friday after speaking in Garden City, N.Y. Photo: Samuel Corum/Getty Images 🐘 Trump finally spending: The RNC has begun privately telling sister committees — NRSC for the Senate and NRCC for the House — that it plans to distribute tens of millions of dollars to each committee for midterm ads and operations, Axios' Alex Isenstadt reports. 

 Why it matters: Republicans were getting antsy as President Trump dragged his feet on releasing the massive war chests at his disposal. CNN reports that he's now given the green light for tens of millions in midterm spending from his personal super PAC, MAGA Inc. 
 
