 8. 🌴 1 for the road: Country-club drama 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Indian Creek Country Club in Biscayne Bay last year. Photo: Anthony Devlin/Getty Images Jeff Bezos and Jared Kushner now know what money can't buy: membership in the country club on Indian Creek island, the "Billionaire Bunker" off Miami Beach where they splashed out for mansions but can't golf.

 "Indian Creek represents one of South Florida's major battlefields where the region's newly minted billionaires are clashing with those of more modest affluence who often trace their Miami roots back generations," The Wall Street Journal reports . Island residence doesn't come with membership, and both tycoons are sucking up to neighbors in an effort to get in, the Journal says: 

 Bezos , who has spent a combined $234 million on three Indian Creek properties, and his wife, Lauren Sánchez Bezos, were special guests in February at the club's annual dock party. "He worked the scene, engaging in small talk with members," but has yet to be offered membership. Kushner and his wife, Ivanka Trump, hosted a housewarming cocktail party at their own compound, but "only about half the [club's] board showed up ... While some board members were out of town, the signal still seemed clear: The Kushners weren't about to get in." The initiation fee at the 350-member club runs close to $1 million. Annual dues are $44,000.

